        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/2333011/items/RYWFRU3L>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/2333011/items/S7ZCUKYB>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/2333011/items/SCWGIZF5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/2333011/items/WJHSEYWW>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/2333011/items/WRGQMMGS>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/2333011/items/ZM6T93MG>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/AMA18-ch3-1526> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:AMA18 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/AMA18-3.1> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Biodiversity loss is known to substantially decrease ecosystem function and stability (Cardinale et al., 2011; O’Connor et al., 2017). Consequently, biodiversity loss and ecosystem degradation diminish the ability of humans to benefit from or establish spiritual relationships with other living beings.

The relationships between biodiversity and ecosystem function have been rigorously investigated in numerous experiments (e.g. Cardinale et al., 2011) and in theoretical (Loreau, 2010; Tilman et al., 1997) and observational studies in a wide range of ecosystems, including grasslands (Grace et al., 2016; Hautier et al., 2014), forests (Gamfeldt et al., 2013; Liang et al., 2016; Paquette & Messier, 2011), drylands (Maestre et al., 2012) and marine systems (Dee et al., 2016; Duffy et al., 2016), many conducted in the Americas. Recent studies have also revealed many potential benefits of increasing plant diversity in managed production systems, including enhancing the production of crops, forage, wood, and fish; stabilizing productivity; enhancing pollinators and pollination; suppressing weeds and other pests; and accumulating and retaining soil nutrients and carbon (Balvanera et al., 2006, 2014; Cardinale et al., 2012; Kremen & Miles, 2012; Letourneau et al., 2011; Quijas et al., 2010; Scherer-Lorenzen, 2014).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.1.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "How is biodiversity linked to ecosystem functions and ecosystem services?" .

